arrange(): sort rows

gapminder |&gt;
  filter(continent == "Europe", year == 2007) |&gt;
  select(country, lifeExp, pop, gdpPercap) |&gt;
  mutate(
    country = as.character(country),
    pop_m = pop / 1e6,
    gdp_bn = pop * gdpPercap / 1e9
  ) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(gdp_bn))

# A tibble: 30 × 6
  country        lifeExp      pop gdpPercap pop_m gdp_bn
  &lt;chr&gt;            &lt;dbl&gt;    &lt;int&gt;     &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;dbl&gt;  &lt;dbl&gt;
1 Germany           79.4 82400996    32170.  82.4  2651.
2 United Kingdom    79.4 60776238    33203.  60.8  2018.
3 France            80.7 61083916    30470.  61.1  1861.
4 Italy             80.5 58147733    28570.  58.1  1661.
5 Spain             80.9 40448191    28821.  40.4  1166.
# ℹ 25 more rows



arrange() sorts ascending, and desc() flips it.

Practice: mutate and arrange, the stretch


Largest economy

gapminder |&gt;
  filter(year == 2007) |&gt;
  mutate(gdp_bn = pop * gdpPercap / 1e9) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(gdp_bn)) |&gt;
  select(country, gdp_bn, gdpPercap)

# A tibble: 142 × 3
  country       gdp_bn gdpPercap
  &lt;fct&gt;          &lt;dbl&gt;     &lt;dbl&gt;
1 United States 12934.    42952.
2 China          6540.     4959.
3 Japan          4035.    31656.
4 India          2723.     2452.
5 Germany        2651.    32170.
# ℹ 137 more rows



Richest per person

gapminder |&gt;
  filter(year == 2007) |&gt;
  mutate(gdp_bn = pop * gdpPercap / 1e9) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(gdpPercap)) |&gt;
  select(country, gdp_bn, gdpPercap)

# A tibble: 142 × 3
  country       gdp_bn gdpPercap
  &lt;fct&gt;          &lt;dbl&gt;     &lt;dbl&gt;
1 Norway          228.    49357.
2 Kuwait          119.    47307.
3 Singapore       215.    47143.
4 United States 12934.    42952.
5 Ireland         167.    40676.
# ℹ 137 more rows




The United States has the largest economy, and China the second largest, at only about 5,000 dollars per person. Norway is the richest per person, although it ranks only 40th by total GDP. Total size and wealth per person are different questions.

summarise(): summary statistics per group

This is a data summary table: step 2 of the workflow in one pipeline.




gapminder |&gt;
  filter(year == 2007) |&gt;
  summarise(
    mean_life = mean(lifeExp),
    n = n()
  )

# A tibble: 1 × 2
  mean_life     n
      &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;int&gt;
1      67.0   142




gapminder |&gt;
  filter(year == 2007) |&gt;
  group_by(continent) |&gt;
  summarise(
    mean_life = mean(lifeExp),
    n = n()
  )

# A tibble: 5 × 3
  continent mean_life     n
  &lt;fct&gt;         &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;int&gt;
1 Africa         54.8    52
2 Americas       73.6    25
3 Asia           70.7    33
4 Europe         77.6    30
5 Oceania        80.7     2




Without groups, summarise() returns one row for the whole table. group_by() changes nothing you can see: it tells summarise() to work once per group. n() counts the rows of each group.
